Subject: [PATCH 10820/11362] The terminal-blocked fix shipped and I verified
 it independently

dbec4cf522 (REQ-CONDUCTOR-FINISHED-1) supersedes my "identified, not shipped" note.
For status blocked only, a trustworthy non-empty verdict now marks the artifact
finished before the bootstrap-status check, which stops the rerun and the
FAIL-counting in one change.

My objection was inverted and the code's own history says so. I argued the fix would
convert recoverable environmental stalls into permanent ones.
_verdict_is_untrustworthy has treated bare blocked_<resource> as trustworthy-terminal
since the 2026-05-27 .294 fix, whose reasoning is recorded as "no retry can change
that", and the Pre-Launch Preconditions Discipline says a blocked task simply
retires, with resource recovery being planner-level work. Retry-when-the-resource-
returns was never the contract.

The natural experiment is clean: exp6898 wrote status complete_blocked and logged OK
while exp6901 wrote status blocked the same day and burned three runs, on identical
verdict shapes. Seven tasks in two weeks each burned exactly three FAILs before
retiring.

Verified here rather than accepted: skeletons with no verdict and with empty
verdicts still re-run, running and partial statuses with pre-written terminal
verdicts still re-run so the exp1028 hole stays closed, and only blocked-plus-honest
-terminal-verdict reads finished. The real exp6901 artifact now reads finished. The
conductor compiles and imports, which matters because it re-execs into this.

The spec's honest limit is carried into the entry: whether a retry would ever have
succeeded cannot be measured retroactively, since a successful retry overwrites the
artifact. Doctrine and seven-for-seven both point one way; neither proves the
counterfactual.

